The `Dashboard` component now follows the same parallel data-fetching pattern as `getParallelData` in `app/page.tsx`.

**Parallel fetching** — All three API calls run concurrently via `Promise.all`, so none of them block the others.

**Resilient fallback** — A `try/catch` returns mock data when requests fail (e.g. at build time or when routes are missing), matching how the page handles external API failures.

**Display** — Analytics metrics, a notifications list, and settings are rendered in separate sections.

```1:56:app/Dashboard.tsx
async function getDashboardData() {
  try {
    const [analytics, notifications, settings] = await Promise.all([
      fetch('/api/analytics').then((r) => r.json()),
      fetch('/api/notifications').then((r) => r.json()),
      fetch('/api/settings').then((r) => r.json()),
    ])

    return { analytics, notifications, settings }
  } catch {
    return {
      analytics: { pageViews: 12400, uniqueVisitors: 3200, bounceRate: 42 },
      notifications: [
        { id: 1, message: 'New user signed up', read: false },
        { id: 2, message: 'Weekly report ready', read: true },
      ],
      settings: { theme: 'light', emailAlerts: true, timezone: 'UTC' },
    }
  }
}

export default async function Dashboard() {
  const { analytics, notifications, settings } = await getDashboardData()
  // ... renders analytics, notifications, and settings sections
}
```

Note: `/api/analytics`, `/api/notifications`, and `/api/settings` route handlers are not in the repo yet, so the component currently shows the mock fallback at runtime. Once those routes exist and return JSON in the expected shape, the live API data will be used automatically. I can add those route handlers if you want.

